+# Context for the ADR drafted from brief.md (#661). Gathered by adr-keeper, 2026-10-19.
+shape: markdown
+number: "039"
+draft_path: .garura/project/issues/_adr/20261019-122431/adr-draft.md
+level: framework
+decided_in: "#661 (Kickoff's plays get memory only from their agents), under Feature #653 and business intent #606; the #661 plan, item 1 and its Log (`.garura/project/issues/661/specs/plan.md`), across three replies of Kapil's on 2026-10-19"
+decided_by_when_accepted: Kapil Viren Ahuja   # leave **Decided by:** empty in the draft
+title_suggestion: "Every Memory Write Goes to Short-Term Memory, a Play Reads No Memory, and the Tool Reads Phoenix Memory Without Copying It"
+voice: >
+  Plain short sentences (ASD-STE100 style), like ADR 036 to 038. One idea per sentence.
+  Keep Kapil's quotes from the brief word for word, typos included. Each decision part ends
+  with the quote that backs it.
+
+# ------------------------------------------------------------------
+supersedes:
+  - adr: "036"
+    file: 036-approved-answer-lands-in-short-term-memory.md
+    part: >
+      in part — §4 "A person's own answer goes into Garura memory", and the lines that carry it:
+      §8's line on ADR 035 §5 ("A person's own answer goes to Garura memory") and its header's
+      line on ADR 034 §2 ("a person's answers to questions are kept in Garura memory").
+      A question asked of a person, and the answer, are kept in the issue's short-term memory.
+    why: >
+      Brief part 1 replaces §4 word for word ("This replaces ADR 036 §4"). The rest of ADR 036
+      stands and agrees with this ADR: §1 (short-term memory is the current issue only), §2
+      (an approved researched answer lands in short-term memory), §3 (promotion happens outside
+      the work), §5, §6, §7 as amended by ADR 037. Its ICE-impact line "a person's own answer
+      goes to Garura memory" falls with §4.
+
+amends:
+  - adr: "035"
+    file: 035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md
+    parts:
+      - part: '§1 "Memory has three tiers"'
+        what: >
+          Garura memory is `.garura/core/memory/`, and the product model (`.garura/product/`) is
+          part of it, not all of it. The person's answers come off the list of what Garura
+          memory holds; they are kept in the issue's short-term memory until a person promotes
+          them. Short-term memory is `.garura/project/issues/<issue>/`.
+      - part: '§2 "Phoenix memory has one home; a project pulls the subset it needs" (as amended by ADR 038)'
+        what: >
+          The tool never copies a Phoenix file into Garura memory. "It pulls more whenever the
+          tool goes up to Phoenix memory and finds something" and "A short list in the project
+          records what it pulled" go. A project's Garura memory is built separately, from only
+          the part it needs, by `initialize` and by promotion. Phoenix memory's own git
+          repository stands.
+      - part: '§3 "Memory is read in order"'
+        what: >
+          The order is named in full: short-term memory, then `.garura/core/memory/`, then the
+          product model, then Phoenix memory. Stopping at the first that answers, and "never
+          blocks a play", stand.
+      - part: '§4 "One tool finds and updates memory"'
+        what: >
+          "Agents and skills ... call one memory tool" becomes: only agents call the tool. A play
+          reads no memory; it calls its sub-agents, and they read memory through the tool and
+          build the context. A skill reads only what its agent puts in short-term memory. A drive
+          follows the same rule. The rest of §4 stands (Claude Code-style search; a move to
+          Griffin changes only the tool).
+      - part: '§5 "Nothing from the model or the web without an approved memory update" (as amended by ADR 036)'
+        what: >
+          The line ADR 036 added, "A person's own answer goes to Garura memory", is replaced
+          (see the supersede of ADR 036 §4). The rest of §5 stands.
+      - part: 'Negative / Risks, "Not decided here": "where in `.garura/` Garura memory lives (#656)"'
+        what: Settled — `.garura/core/memory/`, with the product model at `.garura/product/`.
+  - adr: "034"
+    file: 034-plays-answer-from-memory-inside-a-drive.md
+    parts:
+      - part: '§1 "Inside a drive, a play answers from memory"'
+        what: >
+          The play does not read memory itself. Its sub-agents read memory through the tool and
+          build the answer. The rule that a question is answered from memory stands.
+        judgment_call: >
+          Yes. §1's words "it answers it from memory" are not wrong about where the answer comes
+          from; they are wrong about who reads. A reader of ADR 034 alone would think the play
+          reads memory, so a pointer is worth it.
+      - part: '§2 "What counts as memory" (as amended by ADR 036)'
+        what: >
+          ADR 036's line "a person's answers to questions are kept in Garura memory" becomes:
+          a person's answers are kept in the issue's short-term memory. The rest of §2, as ADR
+          035 and 036 left it, stands.
+  - adr: "038"
+    file: 038-install-brings-no-memory.md
+    parts:
+      - part: '§2 "Phoenix memory arrives the first time the tool needs it"'
+        what: >
+          Phoenix memory's place on the machine is `~/.phoenix/core/memory` (was the default
+          `~/.garura/phoenix-memory`). The download on first need, with no password wait and a
+          time limit, stands. The tool reads Phoenix memory there; it never copies a file from it
+          into the project.
+      - part: '§4 "The memory tool lives in the project"'
+        what: >
+          The tool sits at `.garura/core/tools/memory/` (was `.garura/tools/memory/`), with its
+          settings beside it. The rest of §4 stands.
+      - part: '§5, its line on ADR 017 §1'
+        what: The whitelist gains `.garura/core/tools/`, not `.garura/tools/`.
+      - part: 'Negative / Risks, "Not decided here"'
+        what: >
+          The question of keeping `.garura/tools/` out of git now concerns
+          `.garura/core/tools/`. It stays open.
+  - adr: "017"
+    file: 017-folder-whitelist.md
+    parts:
+      - part: '§1 "Strict `.garura/` folder whitelist" (as amended by ADR 035 and ADR 038)'
+        what: >
+          `core/` holds `config.yaml`, `memory/` (Garura memory) and `tools/` (the memory tool).
+          The whitelist also allows `install-manifest.json`, `defect-report/` (defects reported
+          to Garura and Phoenix) and `user-provided/` (the evidence layer) at the top of
+          `.garura/`. `product/` and `project/issues/` stay. `project/specs/` is not allowed and
+          goes. ADR 038's `.garura/tools/` moves under `core/`. The rest of §1 stands.
+        note: >
+          §1's tree never listed `project/specs/`; the folder exists on disk
+          (`.garura/project/specs/`, empty). The amendment makes the whitelist name what stays
+          and says plainly that `project/specs/` goes.
+  - adr: "009"
+    file: 009-skill-ltm-organizational-knowledge.md
+    parts:
+      - part: 'Rule 3 "LTM paths are stable conventions" (as amended by ADR 035)'
+        what: >
+          ADR 035 said skills find standards through the memory tool. Now a skill never calls
+          the tool. Its agent finds the standards through the tool and puts what the skill needs
+          in short-term memory. Rules 1, 2, 4 and 5 stand.
+        judgment_call: >
+          Yes. ADR 035's pointer on ADR 009 now says something this ADR changes, so ADR 009 gets
+          a direct pointer — the same reason ADR 036 gave for its pointer on ADR 032.
+
+not_affected:
+  - adr: "011"
+    why: >
+      Skills write to short-term memory and the next step reads from it. This ADR keeps that and
+      adds that a skill reads nothing else. No rule of ADR 011 changes.
+  - adr: "015"
+    why: >
+      Already superseded in part by ADR 035. Its Knowledge File Standard stands; nothing here
+      touches it.
+  - adr: "016"
+    why: >
